><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Dhanya Manikya was king of Tripura from about 1490 to 1515 and one of the most powerful rulers of the Manikya dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dhanyakataka"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/dhanyakataka.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Dhanyakataka</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Dhanyakataka was an ancient city on the Krishna river in present-day Andhra Pradesh, India, at modern Dharanikota beside Amaravati.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/dhar-tichitt"><span class="enc-name">Dhar Tichitt</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Dhar Tichitt is an escarpment in south-central Mauritania with extensive remains of stone-built settlements.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dhar-walata"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/dhar-walata.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Dhar Walata</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Dhar Walata is an escarpment in south-eastern Mauritania with archaeological remains of the Tichitt stone-settlement culture.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/dharanidhar-naik"><span class="enc-name">Dharanidhar Naik</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Dharanidhar Naik was a leader of the Bhuyan people of Keonjhar in Odisha, India, who led a revolt against the Raja of Keonjhar in 1891.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dharanikota"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/dharanikota.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Dharanikota</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Dharanikota is a village on the Krishna river in the Guntur region of Andhra Pradesh, India, close to the great Buddhist stupa of Amaravati.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/dharma"><span class="enc-name">Dharma</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">Dharma is a central concept in Indian religions, denoting moral law, duty and righteous conduct.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dharmachakra"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/dharmachakra.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Dharmachakra</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">The Dharmachakra, or Wheel of the Law, is a Buddhist symbol representing the teaching of the Buddha, recalling his first sermon at Sarnath, known as setting the wheel…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dharmakshema"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/dharmakshema.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Dharmakshema</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Dharmakshema was an Indian Buddhist monk and translator who worked in China under the Northern Liang ruler Juqu Mengxun.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/dharmapala-pala-emperor"><span class="enc-name">Dharmapala (Pala emperor)</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Dharmapala was the second ruler of the Pala Empire of Bengal and Bihar, reigning from about 770 to 810.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/dharmapala-of-kotte"><span class="enc-name">Dharmapala of Kotte</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Dharmapala of Kotte was the last king of Kotte in Sri Lanka, placed on the throne as a child in 1551 under Portuguese protection.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/dharmashastra"><span class="enc-name">Dharmashastra</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">Dharmashastra is the body of Sanskrit treatises on dharma, the religious and legal duties of Hindus, of which the Laws of Manu is the best known.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/dharmawangsa"><span class="enc-name">Dharmawangsa</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Dharmawangsa Teguh was king of the Medang kingdom in East Java from about 990 to 1016.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dharwad"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/dharwad.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Dharwad</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Dharwad is a city in northern Karnataka, India, forming a twin city with Hubballi.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/dhatusena"><span class="enc-name">Dhatusena</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Dhatusena was a king of the Anuradhapura Kingdom in Sri Lanka who reigned from about 455 to 473.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/dhaun-castle"><span class="enc-name">Dhaun Castle</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Dhaun Castle, Schloss Dhaun, is a castle above the Simmerbach valley near Hochstetten-Dhaun in Rhineland-Palatinate, Germany, first recorded in the early 13th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dhian-singh"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/dhian-singh.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Dhian Singh</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Dhian Singh was a Dogra noble who served as wazir (chief minister) of the Sikh Empire.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dhiban"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/dhiban.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class=