n class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Dhruvasena II, also called Baladitya, was a king of the Maitraka dynasty of Valabhi in Gujarat in the early 7th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/dhu-ghabat"><span class="enc-name">Dhu Ghabat</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">Dhu Ghabat was the chief god of the oasis of Dedan, modern al-Ula in north-western Arabia, worshipped under the kingdoms of Dedan and Lihyan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/dhu-nuwas"><span class="enc-name">Dhu Nuwas</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Dhu Nuwas was a Jewish king of Himyar in South Arabia in the early sixth century, remembered for his persecution of Christians at Najran.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/dhu-raydan"><span class="enc-name">Dhu Raydan</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">Dhu Raydan, after Raydan, the royal palace at the Himyarite capital Zafar, was the name by which the Himyarite kingdom of south Arabia appears in its inscriptions.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/dhul-nunid-dynasty"><span class="enc-name">Dhul-Nunid dynasty</span><span class="enc-type">State</span><span class="enc-snip">The Dhul-Nunid dynasty (Banu Dhi al-Nun) was a Berber family that ruled the Taifa of Toledo from the 1030s to 1085.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dhulbahante"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/dhulbahante.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Dhulbahante</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Dhulbahante are a Somali clan of the Harti branch of the Darod, living mainly in the Sool region and parts of Sanaag and Togdheer.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/dhvanyaloka"><span class="enc-name">Dhvanyaloka</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Dhvanyaloka ('Light on Suggestion') is a Sanskrit treatise on poetics by the Kashmiri critic Anandavardhana, written in the 9th century under the Utpala dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/di-xin"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/di-xin.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Di Xin</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Di Xin was the last king of the Shang dynasty of China.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/di-yi"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/di-yi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Di Yi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Di Yi was the penultimate king of the Shang dynasty and father of Di Xin, the last Shang king.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/diaba-lompo"><span class="enc-name">Diaba Lompo</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Diaba Lompo is the legendary founder of the Gulmance kingdom of Gulmu, centred on Fada N'Gourma in eastern Burkina Faso.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/diadochi"><span class="enc-name">Diadochi</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Diadochi ('successors') were the generals and companions of Alexander the Great who fought over his empire after his death in 323 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/diaeus"><span class="enc-name">Diaeus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Diaeus was a general of the Achaean League who, with Critolaus, led it into its final war against Rome.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/diagoras-of-rhodes"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/diagoras-of-rhodes.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Diagoras of Rhodes</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Diagoras of Rhodes was a celebrated boxer from Ialysos, whose victory at the Olympic Games of 464 BCE was praised by Pindar in his seventh Olympian ode.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/diaguita"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/diaguita.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Diaguita</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Diaguita were a group of indigenous peoples speaking the Cacán language who lived in the valleys of north-western Argentina and north-central Chile.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/diakhao"><span class="enc-name">Diakhao</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Diakhao is a village in the Fatick Region of western Senegal that served as the royal capital of the Serer kingdom of Sine.</span></a></li></ul>
